---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { destinations, monthRanges, MONTH_SHORT, MONTHS, destMonthUrl, rankForMonth } from '../../lib/data';

const asia = destinations.filter((d) => (d.region === 'Asia' && d.climate === 'tropical') || ['hanoi', 'goa', 'sri-lanka'].includes(d.slug));
const rows = asia
  .map((d) => {
    const byRain = [...d.months].sort((a, b) => b.rain - a.rain);
    return { d, wet: byRain.slice(0, 3).map((m) => m.index), dry: byRain.slice(-3).map((m) => m.index), max: byRain[0] };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => a.d.name.localeCompare(b.d.name));
const julyAug = rankForMonth(6, asia).filter((d) => d.months[6].score >= 65 && d.months[7].score >= 65);
---

<Guide slug="monsoon-season-asia" photo="bali">
  <p>
    "Monsoon" simply means a seasonal shift in the prevailing winds — and with it, a wet season and a dry season. Across
    South and Southeast Asia, the timing varies a lot from place to place, which is good news: <strong>somewhere is almost
    always in its dry season.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    In rough terms, the <b>south-west monsoon</b> brings rain to India's west coast, Sri Lanka's south-west, the Andaman
    coast of Thailand and the Maldives from about May to October. The <b>north-east monsoon</b> brings rain to Sri Lanka's
    east, Malaysia's east coast, Thailand's Gulf islands and central Vietnam from about October to January. Bali and the
    rest of Indonesia follow the southern-hemisphere pattern, with a dry season from April to October.
  </p>

  <h2>Wet and dry months by destination</h2>
  <p>Based on average monthly rainfall from national weather services:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Destination</th><th scope="col">Wettest months</th><th scope="col">Driest months</th><th scope="col">Peak rain</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(({ d, wet, dry, max }) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={d.url}>{d.name}</a></th>
              <td class="left">{monthRanges(wet, MONTH_SHORT)}</td>
              <td class="left">{monthRanges(dry, MONTH_SHORT)}</td>
              <td><a href={destMonthUrl(d, max.index)}>{max.rain} mm ({max.short})</a></td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Where is dry in July and August?</h2>
  <p>
    Summer holidays fall right in the middle of the south-west monsoon, but these destinations rate good or great in both
    July and August:
  </p>
  <ul>{julyAug.map((d) => <li><a href={destMonthUrl(d, 6)}>{d.name}</a> — {d.months[6].rain} mm of rain in July</li>)}</ul>

  <h2>Is the rainy season really that bad?</h2>
  <p>
    Often not. In much of Southeast Asia, wet-season rain comes as short, heavy afternoon downpours, with sunny mornings —
    and you'll find lower prices, greener landscapes and fewer tourists. The exceptions are coasts where the monsoon brings{' '}
    <b>rough seas</b> (boat trips and island hopping get cancelled — think Phuket, Krabi or Palawan in the wet season) and
    regions prone to <b>typhoons and floods</b>, such as central Vietnam in October and November.
  </p>
  <h2>Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Plan island hopping and diving trips for the dry season on that specific coast.</li>
    <li>Keep a buffer day for cancelled boats or flights in typhoon season.</li>
    <li>Pack a light rain jacket and quick-dry clothes rather than an umbrella.</li>
    <li>Check our month pages, e.g. <a href={`/where-to-go-in-${MONTHS[6].toLowerCase()}/`}>where to go in July</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>

<style>
  td.left {
    text-align: left;
    white-space: normal;
  }
</style>
